<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Wall of China</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Great Wall</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Wall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> is a system of fortifications built across northern China over many centuries to defend agrarian states against steppe peoples. Its best-known surviving sections in brick and stone were built or rebuilt under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> as part of its northern frontier defence. The wall's limits as a barrier became clear when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> passed through it and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china.jpg" alt="Great Wall of China" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Severin.stalder · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Great_Wall_of_China_at_Jinshanling-edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chinese were already familiar with wall-building by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a>, between the 8th and 5th centuries BCE. During that time and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> that followed, states such as Zheng, Chu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, Wei, Zhao, Qi and Yan built extensive fortifications to defend their own borders. These were made mostly of stone or of earth and gravel stamped between board frames.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> emerged victorious in 221 BCE, its ruler, the First Emperor, ordered the destruction of the wall sections that divided his empire among the former states. To position the empire against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, however, he had new walls built to connect the remaining fortifications along the northern frontier. The guiding principle was Build and move on, implying that the walls were not a permanently fixed border. Later the Han, the Northern dynasties and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> repaired, rebuilt or expanded sections at great cost, while the Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> undertook no significant effort in the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> made the most substantial contributions after its defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> at the Battle of Tumu. Dynasties founded by non-Han peoples, among them the Northern Wei, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, the Jin and the Western Xia, also built walls, further north than the Han ones. A survey reported in 2012 concluded that the walls and trenches of all periods span 21,196.18 km, of which the Ming wall measures 8,850 km.</p><p>Before the Ming the wall was mainly rammed earth, stone and wood. The Ming used bricks heavily, which were easier to work with and bore more weight, and cut stone for foundations and gateways. Sticky <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> mortar, made of sticky rice soup and slaked lime, held the bricks together. Signal towers on hilltops allowed units to call reinforcements and warn garrisons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The name Long Wall appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s Records of the Grand Historian. Because of the wall's association with the First Emperor's supposed tyranny, later dynasties usually avoided that name for their own additions. Only in the Qing period did it become the general term. The belief that the wall can be seen from the Moon is a myth; an early reference to the claim is a letter of 1754 by the antiquary William Stukeley.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Wall of China” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ming <b>Great Wall</b> seen by visitors today is largely a 16th-century brick-faced work, built after the Zhengtong Emperor was captured by the Oirats at Tumu in 1449.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…former subjects who built a khaganate on the steppe by 402; their raids were constant, and in 423 a wall over 2,000 li (1,080 kilometres) was built from Chicheng in Hebei to Wuyuan in Inner Mongolia, the first <b>Great Wall</b> since the Han, and a second in 446.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Earlier lords' walls were joined along the northern border into the base of the <b>Great Wall of China</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qing Empire originated among the Jurchen people of Manchuria, a confederation of clans living in the forested river valleys north-east of the <b>Great Wall</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Book of Jin names nineteen Xiongnu tribes that resettled within the <b>Great Wall</b>, among them the Chuge, the Qiangqu, the Helai and the Lijie; the Chuge, also called the Xiuchuge, were said to be the most powerful and prestigious.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>These projects, with the extension of the <b>Great Wall</b> and the rebuilding of Luoyang, were run by an efficient bureaucracy but conscripted millions of workers at a heavy human cost.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
